6|0@iamverysmart^1438716404#ctrfsck:faelun$:PhD student in personality psychology here. The Myers Briggs is basically voodoo and no one in academic psychology takes it seriously, or has taken it seriously since the late 60s early 70s. If anyone reading this is actually interested in learning about modern personality theory/assessment I would encourage you to check out Hexaco.org, this is the current gold standard in personality assessment and its FREE to the public! 
0|0@iamverysmart^1438724754#ctrlkf3:Almighteh$:There are a lot of things about that test that I don't like. It seems to illogically link certain traits and actions together. Just as an example, from the information it provided about creativity it says "Low scorers have little inclination for original thought, whereas high scorers actively seek new solutions to problems and express themselves in art." It's linking the desire to "express yourself" in art with the ability to think for one's self and solve problems. I'm pretty young, but I've met at least a few people in my day. The engineering students I've met are the type of people who think for themselves, and the career path they are going into is problem solving as an occupation. They didn't tend to be the type of people who appreciate art, let alone make it for themselves. If one were to take this test seriously then the engineering students would be less likely to think for themselves and not good at problem solving due to their lack of sophistication in art. Conversely, the artists I've met tend to not have an original thought in their head. If their cars broke down then they might make a painting to describe how they feel, but that wouldn't exactly change the tire or get the car running. That's to say, they were overwhelmingly not problem solves. Just to suggest that there's a correlation between making art and being problem solver is, in my mind, completely absurd. This is just one issue of many that I have with this test. I'm not a PhD student in psychology, but allow me to provide you a layman's perspective on it as a whole. You described the Myers Briggs test as voodoo. I view psychology to be sitting *just* above the line of what is or is no voodoo. Sometimes it can reach higher and accurately describe things about us (like the skinner box), but other times it can so far down into the category of voodoo that it's pretty cringe worthy (like freud). In my uneducated opinion, I think psychology students from the future will look at the hexaco test as voodoo just as students today look at they myers briggs test as voodoo. 
